ar of fire  among the american public  and also you have smokey the bear and  very much a educational  paradigm of you know only you can  prevent wildfires and so much of that  wildfire narrative has been at the  expense of indigenous peoples cultures  and being able to use fire that's frank  lake an ecologist for the u.s forest  service who studies climate change  wildland fire ethnoecology and  traditional ecological knowledge he  actually worked on the study looking at  how cultural burns might affect salmon  and he's a member of the kuruk and urak  communities  so i grew up a lot around that tension  between the federal government and local  tribes around environmental and resource  management around the effects of fire  exclusion and fire suppression and heard  the stories oh our people used to burn a  lot more we're dependent upon fire for  healthy wildlife like the deer and elk  we would hunt the connection between  water is sacred and by burning to reduce  the plants or the trees that use up the  water  i take it that most people maybe of your  generation older have not seen very many  cultural burns is that right that's  correct  coming up after the break we'll get into  how one wildfire devastated a carrot  community and meet a woman who's leading  the way in training the next generation  of fire lighters  over the years that cultural burning has  been suppressed wildfires have become  more frequent and destructive  in the 1990s wildfires burned an average  of 3.3 million acres a year  that rate more than doubled in the 2010s  during which wildfires burned an average  of 7.5 million acres a year  many of these impacted areas are in  california and they often hit  communities where native people live  you also have the tribes here who lived  with uh  some of the highest densest levels of  smoke and emissions  where you had severe air quality days  that were so bad that they had to  develop like a new chart for relating  how how dangerous and how thick the  smoke was imagine being an elder with  emphysema or  other heart and lung complications and  then now you have a power outage you  can't run the air purifier you don't  have air conditioning you're breathing  smoke you're a disadvantage a low income  family so you can't be smoke refugees  and go out to the coast or get a hotel  someplace else  you have to stay here and eat it  in the fall of 2020 keely went to happy  camp a town with many kuruk residents it  had just been devastated by the slater  wildfire which burned nearly 200 homes  many of which belonged to kuruk families  i mean even massive trees were  completely torched you know were burned  to cinders and structures were stat only  just just metal was standing there were  tons of vehicles that were just pulled  off the side of the road that there was  nothing left  yeah it was incredible to see the the  level of devastation of it and yeah it  looked like  an apocalypse to come through  keely was traveling with a kuruk friend  who lived there and was seeing it for  the first time in person  when we got out of the car we were  looking at this devastation this burned  out hulk of a wreck of our of the  vehicle and um nothing left of a  structure except some tin  sheets on the ground that were all  burned up and the first thing she did  was she hauled out a bucket from the car  and asked her i was like what's in the  bucket you know and and she started  tossing acorns around and she said  this is an opportunity this is an  opportunity for  uh me to heal the land by planting these  acorns here you know i'm right here in  this place originally there weren't that  many oaks this is a chance for us to  heal the land and  she said also  that in healing land and planting the  acorns and doing cultural burning all of  this is also a chance for me to heal  myself  in your conversations with you know  members of the kuruk and yurak tribes  like how do how do people talk about  fire and what can we learn from these  tribes about how we should think about  fire you know like my friend ron told me  that when he was a kid  you know his grandmother gave him a box  of matches one day when he was out  playing in the yard and told told him to  not come back in until he had used up  the whole box of matches  right and  yeah it's so different she was teaching  him  to not fear it and to also to how to  learn how to deal with fire you know  like clearly she wasn't afraid of the  fire and she knew that it's possible  that it could get out of control but she  would be watching and she would um she  would deal with it you know and so she  knew that by by  teaching him  very young not to fear it and how to  learn how fire works that she would be  creating someone who would keep their  culture going through cultural burning  right yeah that's sort of the antithesis  to like what i i think a lot of people  have heard when they were kids which is  not don't play with matches right  yeah right exactly that's right it's  right up there with running with  scissors  yeah don't row with scissors and don't  play with matches but i would definitely  say um  my karook norak friends would say  otherwise  to be clear keely isn't encouraging kids  to play with matches it's more about  encouraging kids to have a healthy  relationship with fire  even though there's no absolute way to  control fire healy says the damage that  a cultural burn could cause would be  small compared to a wildfire and there  is a greater risk to consider in not  burning the land regularly  when a wildfire does actually go through  and you've missed the chance to stop  that wildfire that wildfire is going to  destroy  way more way more than a cultural burn  ever would in a cultural burn something  gets out of control and you know you  burned out a couple of houses by  accident or something like that when the  wildfire comes through that entire place  is just burning to a cinder there's not  going to be anything left entire  settlements