p and turn into massive wildfires  they've reduced that wildfire threat  because the wildfire burned into a  recent cultural burn and went and was  able to easier to contain  right so the more you break up the high  fuel loading areas and you reduce the  wildfire risk based on your treatments  and your prescribed fires or your  cultural burns the less you have a  wildfire risk actually threatening your  life and property in your community so  that has been demonstrated that you know  conducting multiple cultural burns or  prescribed fires in a year that can help  combat wildfires  it definitely reduces the fuels reduces  the fuel continuity of how much fuel is  there that could burn under the most  severe conditions  moderates it or reduces that fire  intensity and makes it less damaging i'm  just curious you know in the 20 years  that you've been at you know the forest  service have they become a lot more  receptive to indigenous knowledge  very much so even down to the current  administration and the council  environmental qualities direction on  indigenous traditional ecological  knowledge um the usda department of  agriculture's direction on how the  agency the particular forest service and  research and development is going to  respond to that  this year the white house announced that  it was committed to using indigenous  knowledge in policy dealing with the  relationship between humans and the  environment frank currently helps the  forest service run cultural burns  when it comes to the fall time there's  an indicator where usually the infertile  and buggy acorns brown top bad fall  first and then you start to see this  transition of the white top acorns  falling and that's our indicator locally  for burning and i try to time that just  right before the next rainstorm to burn  off clear nice little understory fire  under the acorn trees that reduces the  buggy acorns cleans up the small sticks  and leaves and then you have on that  open charred understory  white top good good acorns falling on  there that are easily obscene and picked  up  keeley actually visited one of frank's  acorn burns in the klamath river valley  you kind of like look over the klamath  river which is a river that's just sort  of like always painted with  rainbows and there's this beautiful  bright green forest full of conifers so  even in the winter time it's it's always  green he recalls what the ground looked  like after the burn was over the whole  ground was this carpet of black you know  kind of this black ash that was  has drawn everywhere and on top of it  was sitting tons of acorns and they were  all beautiful and they were like big and  plump  and none of them had any holes in them  and the thing that frank said was well  they don't have holes in them is because  the cultural fire burned up and killed  all the acorn pests we time it so that  the the acorn weevils and the acorn  moths that normally go down there they  get burned up in that cultural fire so  we time that fire just right so that it  does that and then you know while we  were there we gathered a bunch of acorns  to take home to make into food  frank says that it's important his kids  understand how these fires are connected  to the food they eat and the things they  use in their house my kids see good  acorns they know what a soup tastes like  they know the deer we've hunted the nice  bucks we've shot in the areas that have  been burned they see the basket material  that made the hazel sticks for their  cradle that they were carried in  frank was partially inspired to teach  his kids about fire by visiting  australia  there he visited the martu a group of  aboriginal australians living in the  country's western desert and i got to  see aboriginal kids take a lighter and  go burn off a small patch and then i saw  the teenagers and older ones burn off a  bigger patch and then i got to go out  with the the men and burn off like a  whole big valley like we're talking like  you k